1
00:00:00,080 --> 00:00:02,884
学习以往面试中的软件系统设计。
Learn software system design in past interviews.

2
00:00:03,278 --> 00:00:11,524
本课程提供了一个实用的五步框架，帮助你在压力下构建思维逻辑并应对模糊的需求。
This course provides a practical five-step framework for structuring your thinking and tackling vague requirements under pressure.

3
00:00:11,982 --> 00:00:21,280
此外，课程还会通过真实案例，从零开始设计类似于 Spotify、Booking.com 和 WhatsApp 的系统。
Additionally, the course walks through real-world examples, designing systems similar to Spotify, Booking.com, and WhatsApp from scratch.

4
00:00:21,646 --> 00:00:23,680
本课程由 Heike Simon Yan 开发。
Heike Simon Yan developed this course.

5
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:27,920
如今大多数软件工程职位都需要系统设计知识。
Most software engineering roles today require system design knowledge.

6
00:00:28,080 --> 00:00:34,948
无论是为了实际工作还是为了面试，你都被要求掌握如何设计大规模系统。
Whether it's for the job itself or for interviews, you are expected to know how to design systems at scale.

7
00:00:35,534 --> 00:00:41,412
特别是对于高级和资深职位的申请者，这是公司决策的关键因素之一。
And especially for senior and staff level positions, it's one of the deciding factors for the company.

8
00:00:42,126 --> 00:00:51,588
这就是为什么我整理了这份完整的系统设计面试指南，包含了在系统设计面试中出现频率最高的真实生产系统案例。
That's why I put together this complete system design interview guide with real production system examples that show up the most often in system design interviews.

9
00:00:52,334 --> 00:00:58,084
首先，我们将从框架开始，学习如何构建你的思维、该问哪些澄清问题，
First, we'll start with the framework to know how to structure your thinking, what clarifying questions to ask,

10
00:00:58,560 --> 00:01:04,068
以及如何确保涵盖面试官要求的所有内容且不偏离主题。
and how to make sure you cover everything the interviewer is looking for without going off track.

11
00:01:04,718 --> 00:01:14,212
然后，我们将把同样的框架应用于三个不同的系统设计案例，让你看到同一个框架如何适用于不同的生产系统。
And then we'll apply this same framework across three different system design examples so that you can see how the same framework applies to different production systems.

12
00:01:14,734 --> 00:01:19,332
这是我所知道的通过系统设计面试最实用的框架。
This is the most practical framework that I know for passing system design interviews.

13
00:01:19,790 --> 00:01:30,404
在我职业生涯早期，申请高级职位时我失败了很多次面试，现在回想起来，所有这些面试都有一个共同点，
Early in my career, I failed a lot of these interviews when applying to senior roles, and looking back now, there is one pattern that was the same across all of them,

14
00:01:30,640 --> 00:01:35,012
那就是系统设计几乎总是对话的一部分。
which is system design was almost always part of the conversation.

15
00:01:35,520 --> 00:01:47,236
你可以认为，如今这对于中级职位也是一项要求，尤其是现在所有的软件工程师都使用 AI 来编写代码实现时。
And you can argue that nowadays this is also a requirement for mid-level positions, especially now when all of the software engineers use AI to write the code implementation.

16
00:01:47,502 --> 00:01:56,160
公司想知道你是否理解大局、组件之间如何交互，以及如何从头开始设计解决方案，
Companies would like to know whether you understand the bigger picture and how components talk to each other, how to design solutions from scratch

17
00:01:56,320 --> 00:01:57,796
并讨论其中的权衡。
and discuss the trade-offs.

18
00:01:58,446 --> 00:02:08,419
当然，根据你申请的是中级还是高级职位，知识的深度会有所不同，但无论你是中级、高级还是更高职级，你都必须为这类面试做好准备。
Of course, the depth of knowledge is different depending on whether you apply for mid to senior roles, but still you have to be prepared for this type of interviews,

19
00:02:08,814 --> 00:02:11,360
无论你是中级、高级还是更高级别。
whether you're mid, senior or above.

20
00:02:11,694 --> 00:02:14,560
所以这实际上会以不同的方式呈现。
So this shows up in different ways actually.

21
00:02:14,800 --> 00:02:23,680
我们最常见的一种是白板式面试，即有一个环节让你从头设计一个完整的系统或功能。
The most common one that we know is the whiteboarding style, where there is a session where you design a full system or a feature from scratch.

22
00:02:23,920 --> 00:02:26,308
但实际上还有另外三种类型。
But there are actually three more types to this.

23
00:02:26,798 --> 00:02:35,076
另一种可能是讨论公司现有基础设施中的瓶颈，并看你是否能提出解决方案，
Another way can be discussion about bottlenecks in the company's existing infrastructure and see if you can suggest solutions to this

24
00:02:35,280 --> 00:02:38,340
并且掌握系统设计的基本知识。
and you have the basic knowledge of system design.

25
00:02:38,894 --> 00:02:49,188
另一种类型是回顾你过去的项目，通过对你过去项目的提问来判断你是否真正理解了你所构建系统的架构。
Another type is walking through your past projects, so questions about your past projects to see if you actually understand the architecture of what you built there.

26
00:02:49,774 --> 00:02:56,260
它也可以是概念性问题，用来测试你对系统设计概念知识的深度。
And it can also be conceptual questions that test the depth of your knowledge about system design concepts.

27
00:02:57,102 --> 00:03:07,600
但即使你学习了所有的系统设计概念，你仍然需要一个框架来引导 45 或 60 分钟的讨论，并充分利用这段时间。
But even if you learn all the concepts of system design, you still need a framework to navigate a 45 or 60 minute discussion and make the most out of that time.

28
00:03:07,760 --> 00:03:10,564
这就是我们提供这个框架和指南的原因。
That's exactly why we have this framework and guide.

29
00:03:11,022 --> 00:03:19,588
在进入讨论框架之前，还有一件重要的事情是：大多数工程师认为他们只在他们设计的系统上接受评估，
And another important thing before we'll get to discuss the framework is that most engineers think they are only being evaluated on the system they design,

30
00:03:19,950 --> 00:03:24,068
而这仅仅是你被测试的五个维度中的一个。
which is just one out of five dimensions that you're being tested on.

31
00:03:24,654 --> 00:03:31,236
仅仅了解这些维度，就能极大地帮助你准备这类面试。
And just by knowing what these dimensions are will help you way more to prepare for these type of interviews.

32
00:03:32,046 --> 00:03:41,124
在进入这些维度之前，我们先来了解一下，为什么这比行为面试或实战编程等其他类型的面试更难。
Before getting to the dimensions, let's first understand why this feels hard compared to other type of interviews like behavioral rounds or live coding.

33
00:03:41,614 --> 00:03:50,532
例如，如果是实战编程环节，通常是 LeetCode 风格的问题或者一些现实世界的挑战，但无论哪种情况，
For example, if it's a live coding round, this usually is either a lead code style problem or some real world challenge, but either way,

34
00:03:50,734 --> 00:03:53,280
都有明确的输入和输出，对吧？
there are clear inputs and outputs, right?

35
00:03:53,390 --> 00:04:02,948
假设是一个 LeetCode 风格的挑战，你有一个接受特定参数的函数，你知道这些参数的确切类型，
Let's say if it is a lead code style challenge, then you have a function which accepts these type of arguments, you know exactly what the type of the argument is,

36
00:04:03,310 --> 00:04:07,876
你也清楚函数预期返回什么样的结果。
you know exactly what you're expected to return as an output from the function.

37
00:04:08,238 --> 00:04:14,080
所以你的解决方案是否可行是明确的，因为你可以复制粘贴代码并尝试运行它，
So it is clear whether your solution works or not, because you can copy-paste this code and try to run it,

38
00:04:14,160 --> 00:04:20,964
你甚至可以在白板上跑一些测试用例来核对，而不需要真的去运行代码。
and you can also double check by running test cases on a whiteboard without even running the code.

39
00:04:21,440 --> 00:04:25,476
但系统设计恰恰与这些完全相反。
But system design is actually the opposite of all of that.

40
00:04:25,966 --> 00:04:32,260
首先，需求非常模糊，你的任务是澄清这些需求并确定范围。
First of all, the requirements are very vague, and it is your job to clarify those requirements and lock in the scope.

41
00:04:32,720 --> 00:04:38,468
而且对于输出——也就是你提出的解决方案——并没有唯一的标准答案。
And also the output, which is the solution that you will propose, there is no single right answer to it.

42
00:04:39,182 --> 00:04:47,120
这种面试考察的是你的思维方式，而不仅仅是你掌握的知识，或者你是否能给出一个正确的方案，
And this type of interview is testing how you think, not just what you know, or not whether you can come up with the right solution,

43
00:04:47,280 --> 00:04:52,452
因为在系统设计面试中，你可能会遇到的问题并没有唯一的标准答案。
because there is no single right answer to the question that you will get during system design interview.

44
00:04:52,814 --> 00:04:56,228
基于此，让我们看看你究竟在哪些方面受到评估。
With that, let's see what are you actually being evaluated on.

45
00:04:56,718 --> 00:05:05,120
大多数候选人通过背诵几个架构（比如 Spotify、WhatsApp 等）来做准备，这当然也是一种进步，
Most candidates prepare by just memorizing a couple of architectures like Spotify, WhatsApp, and so on, which is, of course, a step forward

46
00:05:05,280 --> 00:05:16,452
如果你了解几种变体系统以及包含哪些组件，但你还需要理解面试官评分的其他四个维度。
if you know a couple of variants of systems and what components go into this, but you need to also understand the other four dimensions that interviewers score you on.

47
00:05:16,878 --> 00:05:20,804
让我们从第一个开始：沟通与协作。
So let's start with the first one, which is communication and collaboration.

48
00:05:21,294 --> 00:05:29,764
这考察的是你是否会提出澄清性问题，在过程中是否验证假设，还是只是凭空假设并开始构建系统。
This is whether you ask clarifying questions, whether you validate assumptions as you go, or you just make assumptions and start building the system.

49
00:05:30,222 --> 00:05:34,084
它也在某种程度上测试你在真实工作环境中的表现。
And it's kind of testing how you would work in a real-world setup as well.

50
00:05:34,638 --> 00:05:42,276
假设你的经理给你分配了一个任务，比如一个大型功能或一个需要从零开始构建的大型系统服务。
Let's say your manager assigns a task to you, like a big feature or a big system service you need to build from scratch.

51
00:05:42,606 --> 00:05:44,400
在这种情况下你会怎么做？
What do you do in this kind of situations?

52
00:05:44,560 --> 00:05:52,640
如果有些内容不明确，你会直接凭假设去构建方案，最后才发现那并不是他们想要的吗？
If some things are not clear, do you just make assumptions, build a solution, and then figure out that it was not what they were expecting?

53
00:05:52,800 --> 00:06:01,188
这样你就浪费了大量时间，然后你还得重新写一个符合他们要求的方案。
So now you just wasted a lot of time in between and you need to come back and write another solution, which is actually what they were looking for.

54
00:06:01,550 --> 00:06:12,356
还是说你会从一开始就明确这些问题。所以他们也在试图了解和你合作是否容易，除了
Or you clarify this in the beginning. So they are trying to also understand whether it's easy to work with you or not, whether you are good at communication skills apart from

55
00:06:12,974 --> 00:06:14,052
技术知识之外，你的沟通能力是否出色。
technical knowledge.

56
00:06:14,638 --> 00:06:23,364
下一个是，你是否有结构化的思维方式去解决问题，或者你能否将刚拿到的模糊问题
The next one is whether you have structured way of thinking and approaching problems, or can you break down the vague problem that you just got

57
00:06:23,920 --> 00:06:30,240
分解成具体的行动步骤，而不是在各种随机的决策之间跳来跳去。
into specific outlined actions that you will take, or you just jump between random decisions.

58
00:06:30,320 --> 00:06:34,560
你不会在不同的设计、阶段等内容之间反复横跳。
you go back and forth between different designs, phases, and so on.

59
00:06:34,670 --> 00:06:41,520
因此，优秀的候选人通常能展示出即使在问题不明确的情况下也能组织好思路。
So strong candidates here usually show that they can organize their faults even when the problem is not very clear.

60
00:06:41,806 --> 00:06:50,116
他们至少有一套清晰的步骤大纲，并且在每一步中都能清楚当前正在做什么以及下一步要做什么。
They at least have an outline of what are the steps we are going to take, and at every step is clear what we're working on right now and what's up next.

61
00:06:50,862 --> 00:06:53,124
下一个是你的技术判断力。
The next one is your technical judgment.

62
00:06:53,390 --> 00:07:00,292
所以当你选择一个方案，比如数据库、缓存或消息队列时，你能解释你做出这个决定的原因吗？
So when you pick a solution, let's say database, a cache, a message queue, can you explain your decision why?

63
00:07:00,480 --> 00:07:09,604
比如为什么专门选择 SQL 数据库，或者为什么选择某种缓存机制或特定类型的消息队列。
Like why you chose specifically SQL database, why you chose the caching mechanism or the specific type of message queue that you chose.

64
00:07:10,478 --> 00:07:21,280
这部分展示的是：如果你在系统中选择某些工具，是基于需求来选择的，而不是出于习惯，或者仅仅因为你知道什么是 Kafka。
This part shows that if you choose some tools to be used in a system, you choose them based on the requirements, not just out of habit or not just because you know what Kafka is,

65
00:07:21,440 --> 00:07:25,476
因为可能还有另一种更适合这种系统的消息队列。
but maybe there was another message queue that was a better fit for this type of system.

66
00:07:26,190 --> 00:07:29,380
第四个维度是你的实战深度。
The fourth dimension is your practical depth.

67
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:40,260
这部分能区分出两个拥有相同理论知识的候选人，其中一个还具备构建这类系统的实践经验。
This part differentiates between two candidates who might have same theoretical knowledge, but one of them also has practice with building these type of systems.

68
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:51,012
通过观察你是否考虑故障模式、规模以及系统中可能出现的问题，就能体现出来，因为如果你只是在理论层面学习的话，
So it will show up by seeing whether you think about failure modes, about the scale, about things that can go wrong in the system, because if you just learned at the theory level,

69
00:07:51,520 --> 00:07:55,364
你就无法发现系统中的这些故障。
you wouldn't be able to catch these type of failures in the system.

70
00:07:55,918 --> 00:08:05,348
所以这部分专门用于面试官辨别那些真正交付过生产系统的工程师，还是仅仅从理论上学习的人。
So this part specifically is for interviewers to spot engineers who have actually shipped production systems versus those who just learned from theory.

71
00:08:05,934 --> 00:08:12,676
最后一点是时间管理，因为这类面试通常是 45 或 60 分钟。
And then lastly, it's the time management as well, because these type of interviews are usually either 45 or 60 minutes,

72
00:08:13,200 --> 00:08:20,160
你不能卡在某一个环节，或者因为只做高层设计而耗尽了所有时间。
and here you need to not get stuck on one area and run out of time by doing high-level design only.

73
00:08:20,398 --> 00:08:31,332
值得注意的是，你应该尽早询问面试官他们想重点关注哪里，或者由你主动提议并达成共识，以确保你能有效地管理时间。
And it's worth asking your interviewer early where they want to focus on, or proposing it yourself and getting alignment to make sure that you're managing your time effectively.

74
00:08:31,600 --> 00:08:40,480
在整个面试过程中，你需要时刻清楚现在是什么时间、还剩多少时间，以及你在这个环节上花了多少时间。
And along the process of an interview, you need to always know what's the time and how much is left, how much you spent on this section,

75
00:08:40,640 --> 00:08:46,800
如果你在某个环节花费了大量时间，就该继续往下走了。所以你也需要展示出时间管理能力，这再次体现了……
and if you're spending a lot of time on it, it's time to move on. So you need to also show time management, which again.

76
00:08:47,598 --> 00:08:53,188
这五个支柱都对应着你如何在真实环境中工作。
All of these five pillars translate to how you will work in a real environment.

77
00:08:53,486 --> 00:08:59,140
如果你在面试中无法管理好时间，那么你在工作中也无法很好地管理时间。
If you can't manage your time during an interview, then you're also not good at managing your time during work.

78
00:08:59,630 --> 00:09:10,180
如果你没有实战深度，如果你只懂理论，你就无法自信地交付项目，对吧？这五个支柱都是如此。
If you don't have practical depth, then you also won't be able to ship projects confidently if you only know the theory, right? And same applies to all of these five pillars.

79
00:09:10,894 --> 00:09:21,188
既然我们了解了这五个支柱，现在让我们重点关注时间管理部分，以及完成一个系统设计方案需要经历哪些阶段。
And now that we know these five pillars, let's focus on the time management part and what are the stages you need to take to complete a system design solution.

80
00:09:21,518 --> 00:09:24,516
任何系统设计问题都有五个步骤。
There are five steps for any system design problem.

81
00:09:24,974 --> 00:09:29,892
这是一个可以应用于任何白板式系统设计面试的重复性结构。
This is a repeatable structure you can apply to any whiteboarding system design interview.

82
00:09:30,096 --> 00:09:37,892
这里的目标是保持条理清晰，并在这 45 或 60 分钟里向面试官展示你思考问题的逻辑。
And the goal here is to stay organized and show the interviewer how you think through the problems throughout these 45 or 60 minutes.

83
00:09:38,702 --> 00:09:43,696
60 分钟其实更常见，因为这类面试需要涵盖的内容很多。
60 minutes is actually more common because there is a lot to cover in this type of interviews.

84
00:09:43,776 --> 00:09:49,732
假设是 60 分钟，让我们看看我们将采取哪些步骤，以及为每个步骤分配多少时间。
So assuming it's 60 minutes, let's see what are the steps we will take and how much time we will dedicate to each step.

85
00:09:50,478 --> 00:09:53,252
第一个就是明确需求。
The very first one is clarifying requirements.

86
00:09:53,742 --> 00:09:57,540
在理解范围之前，你不想开始设计任何东西。
You don't want to start designing anything before you understand the scope.

87
00:09:57,806 --> 00:10:00,496
这是候选人最常犯的错误。
And this is the most common mistake candidates make.

88
00:10:00,718 --> 00:10:06,084
让我们先从高层来看看这五个步骤是什么，然后我们将详细讨论每一个。
So let's see what these five steps are at a high level, and then we'll get to discuss each of them in more detail.

89
00:10:06,606 --> 00:10:14,176
第一个是，你需要花至少 5 到 10 分钟来明确需求，具体取决于面试的时长。
The very first one is you need to clarify requirements at least for 5 to 10 minutes, depending on the length of the interview.

90
00:10:14,336 --> 00:10:20,964
在进入任何设计之前，你必须确保掌握了所有的功能性需求和非功能性需求。
You need to make sure you have all the functional and non-functional requirements before moving on to designing anything.

91
00:10:21,454 --> 00:10:30,736
之后，你可以先从数据和 API 层开始，然后再进入高层架构，或者这部分可以灵活变动。
After that, you can either start by data and API layer and then get into the high-level architecture, or this part is kind of variable.

92
00:10:30,816 --> 00:10:37,892
你也可以先从架构开始，然后再进入数据和 API 层，哪种方式让你觉得更轻松就选哪种。
You can also start from architecture and get into the data and API layer afterwards, whichever feels easier for you.

93
00:10:38,574 --> 00:10:49,696
每一部分都要花 15 到 20 分钟，不要超过这个时间，等你有了高层架构、
For each of these, you would spend 15 to 20 minutes, not more, and then you would move on to discussing scale and edge cases towards the end once you have the high-level architecture,

94
00:10:49,856 --> 00:10:51,652
数据层和 API 后，
the data layer and APIs.

95
00:10:51,936 --> 00:11:03,780
再进入最后一部分讨论如何扩展系统，或者如果这类系统的可靠性更重要，你就会讨论其他的边缘情况以及如何处理故障。
So here you might discuss either how you would scale this system, or if reliability is more important for that type of system, you would discuss other edge cases and how you would handle the failures.

96
00:11:04,302 --> 00:11:12,256
最后一部分是总结，并谈论那些你想改进但没时间做的部分。
And the last part is to wrap up and talk about anything that you could improve on this system, but you didn't have time for it.

97
00:11:12,366 --> 00:11:15,876
所以在面试结束前简单提一下这些内容。
So just briefly mentioning this before wrapping up the interview.

98
00:11:16,558 --> 00:11:25,444
让我们从第一步开始，这通常耗时 10 分钟或更短，这是在进入解决方案之前先明确问题的地方。
Let's start with the first step, which usually takes up to 10 minutes or less, and this is where you clarify the problem first before getting into the solution.

99
00:11:25,742 --> 00:11:32,836
在明确功能性需求和非功能性需求之前，你千万不要急于开始设计任何东西。
You do not want to jump into designing anything yet before clarifying the functional and non-functional requirements.

100
00:11:33,678 --> 00:11:41,860
所谓功能性需求，是指我们正在构建的这个系统的主要用例是什么，哪些内容明确超出范围。
By functional requirements, we mean what are the main use cases of this system that we're building, what is explicitly out of scope,

101
00:11:42,176 --> 00:11:48,516
因为如果你被要求设计像 Spotify 这样的产品，你不可能在 60 分钟内完成。
because if you're given something to design like Spotify, you can never design it in 60 minutes.

102
00:11:48,656 --> 00:11:56,416
通常需要多个团队、数月的努力，甚至数年时间才能构建并设计出所有的功能，对吧？
This usually takes multiple teams, months of efforts, if not years, to build and design all of this, right?

103
00:11:56,576 --> 00:12:00,132
所以你当然不可能在 60 分钟内作为一名工程师完成它。
So of course you can't do it as a single engineer during 60 minutes.

104
00:12:00,590 --> 00:12:09,476
这里的目标是确定我们关注的主要用例是什么，哪些内容超出范围，这也有助于明确用户是谁
The goal here is to see what are the main use cases we are focusing on, what we leave out of scope, and it also helps to clarify who are the users

105
00:12:09,856 --> 00:12:11,908
以及我们要构建什么样的平台。
and what type of platform we are building.

106
00:12:12,590 --> 00:12:18,576
然后你还要关注非功能性需求，这决定了该系统的约束条件。
Then you also focus on non-functional requirements, which answers what are the constraints for this system.

107
00:12:18,830 --> 00:12:28,196
例如，你将有多少日活或月活用户，读写比是多少，你要明确延迟要求，
For example, how many daily or monthly active users you will have, what is the read-to-write ratio, you would clarify the latency requirements,

108
00:12:28,416 --> 00:12:35,364
还要确定可接受的停机时间，也就是系统的可用性，然后再进入下一阶段。
and also what is the acceptable downtime, which refers to availability of the system, before moving on to the next stage.

109
00:12:35,982 --> 00:12:42,016
而且在每个阶段结束时，最好确认一下是否可以进入下一阶段。
And it's a good idea to always check at the end of each phase whether you're good to move on.

110
00:12:42,096 --> 00:12:46,916
因为如果你在这一阶段漏掉了某些内容，面试官可以给你提示。
Because if you missed some things during this stage, interviewer can give you hints.

111
00:12:47,150 --> 00:12:52,068
例如，你可能没能澄清一些重要的功能性需求。
For example, you may fail to clarify some of the important functional requirements.

112
00:12:52,878 --> 00:13:00,484
这是一个询问「在进入高层设计之前，这些范围和要求是否合理？」的好时机。
This is a good place to ask something like Does this scope and these requirements make sense before I move forward to high level design?

113
00:13:00,816 --> 00:13:03,268
如果他们说是的，那么你就把所有内容都搞清楚了。
If they say yes, then you clarified everything.

114
00:13:03,566 --> 00:13:12,196
如果他们给你提示，通常意味着你在功能性或非功能性需求中漏掉了某些内容，所以在进入下一阶段前你需要先澄清这些。
If they give you a hint, then usually you missed something in functional and non-functional requirements, so you would clarify that before moving on to the next stage.

115
00:13:12,750 --> 00:13:20,096
接下来是第二步，即数据和 API 层或高层设计，取决于哪一个对你来说更容易。
And next is step two, which is either the data and API layer or high-level design, depending on which one is easier for you.

116
00:13:20,256 --> 00:13:29,092
如果你觉得从数据层开始、理解实体如何相互连接，然后再进入高层设计更有帮助，那就采用这种方法。
If you think it helps to start with data layers, understand how entities connect to each other and then get to the high-level design, that's the approach to take.

117
00:13:29,390 --> 00:13:35,716
如果你认为先构思高层架构有助于你理解用户流，那么你就从高层设计开始。
If you think imagining it high level first helps you understand the user flow, then you start with high-level design.

118
00:13:36,096 --> 00:13:42,628
我个人是先从高层设计开始，然后再到这一步，但有时我也从数据和 API 层开始。
I personally start with high-level design, then get here, but sometimes I also start with data and API layer.

119
00:13:42,976 --> 00:13:48,004
这部分是为了对你存储的内容以及客户端如何与你的系统交互进行建模。
This part is to model what you're storing and how clients will interact with your system.

120
00:13:48,686 --> 00:13:54,596
当涉及到数据模型时，我们应该关注这类系统的主要实体是什么。
When it comes to data model, we should focus on what are the main entities for this type of system.

121
00:13:54,990 --> 00:14:00,260
假设是像 YouTube 这样的系统，那么主要实体就是视频和用户。
Let's say if it's something like YouTube, then the main entities are videos and users.

122
00:14:00,750 --> 00:14:08,996
你还需要梳理它们之间的关系，包括数据库中要使用的主键、外键和关键索引，
And you would also map out the relationship between them, what are the primary keys, foreign keys, key indexes that you will use in the database,

123
00:14:09,456 --> 00:14:19,300
以及每条记录的大小。这也是明确哪些数据需要强一致性，哪些可以接受最终一致性的好机会。
and how big each record can get. This is also a good place to clarify which data needs strong consistency and where you can afford eventual consistency.

124
00:14:19,918 --> 00:14:27,876
这一阶段的下一步是定义 API 契约，比如主要的端点以及请求和响应的格式。
Next part of this stage is to also define the API contracts, like what are the main endpoints with request and response formats.

125
00:14:28,430 --> 00:14:40,004
这里如果你能谈到分页、错误处理、限流、版本控制，并且遵循 REST API 的最佳实践（如果你使用的是 REST 类型 API 的话），那就很好，
Here it will also be good if you can talk about pagination, error handling, rate limiting, versioning, and also follow the best practices of REST API in case you're using REST type of APIs,

126
00:14:40,176 --> 00:14:42,468
而在大多数情况下，确实是这种类型。
which is in most cases that's the case.

127
00:14:43,214 --> 00:14:51,056
这也是你做出数据库选择的地方，无论是 PostgreSQL、DynamoDB、MongoDB，还是你选的任何其他东西。
And this is also where you will make the database choice, whether it's PostgreSQL, DynamoDB, MongoDB, whatever you pick.

128
00:14:51,216 --> 00:14:59,376
你还应该解释为什么根据需求选择了那个特定的选项，并阐述其中的权衡，
You should also explain why, given the requirements, you went with that specific option and also walk through the trade-offs

129
00:14:59,536 --> 00:15:02,564
即你考虑过但最终没选择的其他方案。
of what are other things you considered but you didn't choose.

130
00:15:03,182 --> 00:15:07,844
另一个重要部分是高层架构，这大约需要 15 分钟。
The other important part is the high-level architecture, which takes another 15 minutes.

131
00:15:08,718 --> 00:15:15,204
这是你会花大量时间的部分：设计各个组件并展示数据如何在其中流动。
This is where you spend a big portion of a time, design the components and show how data flows through them.

132
00:15:15,918 --> 00:15:24,260
如果你一时没头绪，可以从这四个几乎存在于大多数系统中的组件开始。
In case nothing comes to your mind, you can start with these four components which are almost always present in majority types of systems.

133
00:15:24,846 --> 00:15:34,948
这些是接收请求的 API 服务器、在它们之间分配流量的负载均衡器、持久化数据的数据库，
These are API servers which will be accepting the requests, a load balancer that distributes traffic between them, database where you persist the data,

134
00:15:35,296 --> 00:15:42,308
以及可选的缓存层，用于缓存从数据库中获取的一些常用数据。
and optionally cache layers that caches some common data that you're fetching from the database.

135
00:15:43,150 --> 00:15:50,628
在这一步，梳理从前端到后端再到数据库的用户旅程通常很有帮助。
At this step, it usually helps to map out the user journey from front end to backend all the way to the database.

136
00:15:50,976 --> 00:15:52,896
所以，前端是什么？
So was the front end?

137
00:15:53,134 --> 00:16:03,076
它可能是某种 Web 应用、移动端应用或桌面端应用，然后它连接到你的 API 网关和负载均衡器，由它们分发到各个 API 服务器。
It's some sort of web app or a mobile app or desktop app, and then it connects to your API gateway and load balancers, which will distribute among API servers.

138
00:16:03,502 --> 00:16:06,372
在这些服务器之间你可能会有缓存层。
You might have caching layer among these servers.

139
00:16:07,214 --> 00:16:15,268
可选地，你可能拥有解耦服务的消息队列，并且在后端几乎总是有一个数据库来存储数据。
Optionally, you may have message queues that decouple the services, and you almost always have a database to store the data in the backend.

140
00:16:15,598 --> 00:16:24,336
所以你要带面试官走一遍请求如何流经你的系统：从客户端到负载均衡器、API 服务器，进入缓存和数据库，
So walk the interviewer through how a request moves through your system from client through load balancer, API server into cache and database

141
00:16:24,590 --> 00:16:27,616
以及所有的读写操作。
and back all the read and write operations.

142
00:16:27,918 --> 00:16:33,988
如果你使用了缓存，确保也要提到 生存时间 以及你使用的失效机制。
If you're using caching, make sure to also mention the TTL and what invalidation mechanism you use.

143
00:16:34,414 --> 00:16:43,044
对于系统的不同部分，你还应该阐述你在一致性、可用性和分区容错性之间的权衡选择，
And for different parts of your system, you should also walk through your choice between consistency, availability, and partition tolerance,

144
00:16:43,376 --> 00:16:45,572
这就是源于 CAP 定理的内容。
which is coming from the CAP theorem.

145
00:16:46,254 --> 00:16:57,188
这意味着你的某些组件可能需要不同的处理方法，解释这一点能证明你理解该定理以及你在该系统中做出的权衡。
Which means some of your components may need different approaches, and explaining that shows that you understand the theorem and also the trade-offs that you're making for this system.

146
00:16:57,902 --> 00:17:10,116
第四步是讨论瓶颈、扩展性和可靠性，这部分根据你在高层架构、数据和 API 层上花费的时间多少，大约需要 5 到 10 分钟。
The fourth step is discussing bottlenecks, scale and reliability, which can be anywhere from 5 to 10 minutes depending on how much time you spend on high level design, data, and API layer.

147
00:17:10,702 --> 00:17:18,564
这是非常重要的一部分，它能展示你能考虑到在高负载下哪些地方会出问题，而不仅仅是考虑快乐路径。
This is one of the important parts where you show that you can think about what breaks under heavy load, not just the happy paths.

148
00:17:19,056 --> 00:17:26,116
这也是区分中级候选人和高级候选人的最明显信号之一。
And this is also one of the clearest signals that separates who's a mid level candidate here, who's a senior level candidate.

149
00:17:26,733 --> 00:17:30,084
在这里你会讨论诸如单点故障等问题。
This is where you discuss things like single points of failure.

150
00:17:30,352 --> 00:17:35,972
你要找出这些问题，并提出我们可以做些什么来修复这些单点故障。
You find them and propose solutions on what we can do to fix this single point of failure.

151
00:17:36,430 --> 00:17:39,460
这里就是你要讨论降级方案和故障处理的地方。
This is where you will talk about fallbacks and failures.

152
00:17:39,822 --> 00:17:47,108
例如，当出现问题时，你是否有机制可以回退到一个速度较慢但仍能正常运行的路径。
For example, whether you have mechanisms to fall back to a slower but functional path in case something goes wrong.

153
00:17:47,470 --> 00:17:55,428
在这里，你还可以讨论为了高可用性而采取的冗余措施，比如多可用区副本，
Here you can also expect to talk about redundancy like multi-availability zone replicas for high availability,

154
00:17:55,792 --> 00:18:03,748
跨可用区的应用服务器自动扩容、带复制功能的分布式缓存等等。
application servers to auto-scale across availability zones, distributed caching with replication, and so on.

155
00:18:04,334 --> 00:18:11,072
另一个重点是，你是否能看出该系统将如何进行监控、日志记录和告警。
Another point is whether you can spot how you will be monitoring, logging, and alerting issues for this system.

156
00:18:11,232 --> 00:18:16,356
这部分经常被忽略，但如果你能考虑到这些，就是具备高级工程师思维的强有力信号。
This part is often overlooked, but it's a strong signal that you think like a senior engineer.

157
00:18:16,686 --> 00:18:24,752
这里你还要涵盖 API 延迟、错误率和阈值，以及你会为这类系统设置哪些告警，
This is where you would cover API latency as well, error rates and thresholds, what alerts you will set up for this type of system,

158
00:18:24,912 --> 00:18:25,380
等等。
and so on.

159
00:18:25,998 --> 00:18:34,180
再强调一下，最好总是与面试官确认是否存在特定的故障场景，例如在讨论故障时，
And again, it is always a good idea to check in with the interviewer whether there are specific failure scenarios, for example, if you were discussing failures

160
00:18:34,672 --> 00:18:36,740
询问他们是否希望你重点处理某些特定情况。
that they would like you to address.

161
00:18:36,974 --> 00:18:44,228
高层设计、数据库和 API 设计也是同理，确认你是否涵盖了他们要求的所有内容。
And same goes for high-level design, whether you covered everything they were looking for, and same goes for database and API design.

162
00:18:44,910 --> 00:18:49,028
最后一部分是讨论权衡和扩展。
The last part is where you can expect to discuss trade-offs and extensions.

163
00:18:49,312 --> 00:18:56,004
所以这可能是一个开放式问题，比如你能否发现系统中可以改进的地方。
So this might be an open-ended question, like whether you can spot some things that can be improved in the system.

164
00:18:56,462 --> 00:19:06,564
你通常在最后总结你做出的关键决策、为该系统优先考虑了什么，以及为了实现这些而牺牲了什么，因为系统设计始终关乎权衡，
You usually wrap up by summarizing the key decisions you made, what you prioritized for this system, what you sacrificed because it's always about trade-offs in system design,

165
00:19:07,072 --> 00:19:11,780
而且你必须明确说明这些牺牲背后的原因。
and you should always be explicit about the why behind the sacrifices.

166
00:19:12,558 --> 00:19:18,372
这一部分也是为了看你会不会为未来提出一些可能的扩展方案。
And this part is also to see whether you will propose some possible extensions for the future.

167
00:19:18,958 --> 00:19:23,876
例如，如果你有更多时间，且负载增加到 10 倍或更多时，
For example, if you had more time and the load scaled to 10x or more,

168
00:19:24,526 --> 00:19:27,972
你会采取什么措施来扩展这个服务或系统？
what would you do to extend this service or system?

169
00:19:28,430 --> 00:19:36,740
这样做是为了展示你的思考超出了当前讨论的问题。比如：如果我们扩大规模会怎样？如果出故障了怎么办？
So this is for you to show that you're thinking beyond the problem that was discussed here. So, what if we scale this? What if something goes wrong?

170
00:19:37,166 --> 00:19:41,872
记住，你不可能在 60 分钟内给出一个完美的方案。
And remember that you cannot propose a perfect design in 60 minutes.

171
00:19:41,952 --> 00:19:45,060
所以，当然总会有可以改进的地方。
So, of course, there are always things that you can improve.

172
00:19:45,518 --> 00:19:50,436
这里的目的不是检查你的设计是否完美，因为它不可能完美。
The goal here is not to check whether your design was perfect or not, because it is not.

173
00:19:50,832 --> 00:19:57,796
你只需要展示出如果你有更多时间，你可以对这个系统进行扩展，并且你有更多改进它的想法。
You just need to show that you could extend this if you had more time, you had more ideas on how to improve this system.

174
00:19:58,478 --> 00:20:06,340
这就是管理时间的五个步骤，但让我们回到评估你的这五个维度上，
Now that's the five step system to manage your time, but let's also come back to these five dimensions that you're being evaluated on

175
00:20:06,766 --> 00:20:11,524
看看在面试过程中你应该做什么，以及应该避免做什么。
and see what you should do and what you should avoid doing during the interview.

176
00:20:12,014 --> 00:20:16,592
所以我建议你做的第一件事是：在设计之前提出澄清问题。
So, first thing I recommend you do is ask clarifying questions before designing.

177
00:20:16,752 --> 00:20:17,956
这是第一步。
This is the first step.

178
00:20:18,272 --> 00:20:25,988
如果你在还没搞清楚需求范围之前就直接跳到解决方案，那对面试官来说就是一个警示信号。
So if you proceed, jump into a solution before understanding the scope, that is already a red flag for the interviewer.

179
00:20:26,112 --> 00:20:30,948
所以，在开始设计任何东西之前，这都是一个必须执行的步骤。
So always this is a mandatory step before you get into designing anything.

180
00:20:31,470 --> 00:20:36,068
我发现的另一个常见问题是，工程师往往会陷入这些极端情况中的某一种。
Another common issue I see is engineers get stuck into one of these extremes.

181
00:20:36,432 --> 00:20:48,752
他们要么保持沉默，在不与面试官交流的情况下画图，要么就是一直不停地说话，试图填补所有的空白，却不邀请面试官
They either stay silent and draw diagrams without talking to the interviewer, or they talk all the time and try to fill in all the silence without inviting the interviewer

182
00:20:48,992 --> 00:20:49,828
来提供他们的意见。
for their opinion.

183
00:20:50,478 --> 00:20:54,308
你真正应该做的是把面试官当成你的同事。
What you should do instead is treat the interviewer as your colleague.

184
00:20:54,672 --> 00:21:00,992
这就像是同一家公司的两个软件工程师在共同商讨一个解决方案。
This is like two software engineers in the same company who are working on a solution together.

185
00:21:01,230 --> 00:21:10,564
所以在做出每一个重大决策或进入每一个阶段后，最好先确认一下他们是否满意这个部分的内容。
So after each major decision or each of the phases, it's a good idea to first double check whether this is what they were looking to get out of this part.

186
00:21:11,232 --> 00:21:20,112
如果你遗漏了什么，比如在明确功能性需求时，还有一些你没问到但其实属于系统的一部分的东西，
And if you missed anything, for example, if it was functional requirements that you were clarifying, whether there are some more things that you didn't ask for,

187
00:21:20,654 --> 00:21:23,204
那么这些内容仍然是预期的。
but those are still expected to be part of the system.

188
00:21:23,712 --> 00:21:32,324
另一件事是，在做出决定前总是提供多种方案，尤其是当有多种处理方式时。
The other thing is always suggest multiple approaches before committing, especially when there are multiple ways to go about things.

189
00:21:32,592 --> 00:21:41,952
例如，如果你选择了 SQL，即使这是一个非常明显的选择，比如是一个支付系统，
For example, if you chose SQL, and even if it's a very obvious choice, for example, it was let's say payment system

190
00:21:42,352 --> 00:21:45,552
在这种情况下一致性是最重要的。
where consistency is the most important thing.

191
00:21:46,286 --> 00:21:50,656
如果你在不解释原因的情况下选择 SQL，我们就会认为你不知道原因。
If you choose SQL without explaining why, we just assume you didn't know.

192
00:21:50,830 --> 00:21:57,124
或者面试官随后不得不追问你为什么选 SQL，但他们可能也不会去问。
Or the interviewer then has to follow up and ask a question why you chose SQL, but they may also not.

193
00:21:57,376 --> 00:22:06,020
所以如果你不解释，他们就会默认你不会；如果你选了 SQL，还是要提一下为什么没用 NoSQL 之类的东西。
So if you don't explain, they would assume you don't know. If you chose SQL, still mention why you didn't go with something like No SQL.

